<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Teutonic Order Mergentheim</h1><p class="st-meta">1525 CE – 1809 CE · capital Bad Mergentheim</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Teutonic Order's headquarters at Mergentheim, from which, after losing Prussia in 1525, it governed its scattered lands for nearly three centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia">Grand Master Albert</a> secularised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1525, the order's German master, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-von-cronberg">Walter von Cronberg</a>, who resided at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-mergentheim">Mergentheim</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, was made administrator of the grand mastership in 1527, and his successors combined both offices as Hoch- und Deutschmeister. Mergentheim became the order's seat and the capital of its territories within the empire.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The grand master ranked as a prince of the empire and ruled the district around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-mergentheim">Mergentheim</a> as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> grouped into bailiwicks across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and beyond. The knights, nobles bound by vows, served as officers in imperial armies against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. From the late 16th century the office usually went to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> archdukes or Wittelsbach princes, and Mergentheim castle was rebuilt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> dissolved the order in the states of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> in 1809, and Württemberg annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-mergentheim">Mergentheim</a>, suppressing a local uprising. The order survived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and since 1929 has continued as a clerical order with its seat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/teutonic-order-mergentheim.jpg" alt="Teutonic Order Mergentheim" width="320" height="334"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Template_-_Grand_Master_of_the_Teutonic_Order.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1525 CE – 1809 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bad Mergentheim</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Walter von Cronberg, Maximilian III of Austria, Clemens August of Bavaria</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Grand Mastership of the Teutonic Order, Hoch- und Deutschmeister</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1667&amp;focus=teutonic-order-mergentheim" aria-label="Teutonic Order Mergentheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4905 453 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Teutonic Order Mergentheim lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5225 709L5451 561M5225 709L5229 694M5225 709L5212 730"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5451 561h0M5229 694h0M5212 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5225 709h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5225 709h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bad Mergentheim. <a href="/atlas?year=1667&amp;focus=teutonic-order-mergentheim">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
